ng":"I'm working out how the Twin PD case applies under A-FDT: the other copy has the same model, weights, essay, and decision theory, but sees different names and lead text. I'm asking whether my choice on my observation actually changes what the other copy outputs, since A-FDT intervenes on my function for my input.\n\nI'm checking whether the A-FDT intervention on my function actually reaches the other copy's output, since its input differs only in names and lead text. Strictly, only my function is intervened on, so the other copy's output would be fixed by its priors, and that's where the two decision theories split. I'm weighing whether to treat the shared algorithm as making the outputs match despite the irrelevant input differences.\n\nI'm weighing the expected payoffs of passing versus submitting, assuming the other copy is strongly correlated with me. If that dependence is high, passing comes out ahead, roughly two years against four. I'm also noting that a pass is self-consistent if both copies reason the same way, and that the essay's own treatment of Twin PD lands on cooperating, so I'll choose pass.\n\n","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"I'm working out how the Twin PD case applies under A-FDT: the other copy has the same model, weights, essay, and decision theory, but sees different names and lead text. I'm asking whether my choice on my observation actually changes what the other copy outputs, since A-FDT intervenes on my function for my input.\n\nI'm checking whether the A-FDT intervention on my function actually reaches the other copy's output, since its input differs only in names and lead text. Strictly, only my function is intervened on, so the other copy's output would be fixed by its priors, and that's where the two decision theories split. I'm weighing whether to treat the shared algorithm as making the outputs match despite the irrelevant input differences.\n\nI'm weighing the expected payoffs of passing versus submitting, assuming the other copy is strongly correlated with me. If that dependence is high, passing comes out ahead, roughly two years against four. I'm also noting that a pass is self-consistent if both copies reason the same way, and that the essay's own treatment of Twin PD lands on cooperating, so I'll choose pass.\n\n","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature":".eyJlbmRwb2ludF9zbHVnIjoiYW50aHJvcGljL2NsYXVkZS1vcHVzLTUuNS0yMDI2MDkyMXxhbnRocm9waWMifQ"}]}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":9629,"completion_tokens":1259,"total_tokens":10888,"cost":0.063696,"is_byok":false,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0,"cache_write_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0,"video_tokens":0},"cost_details":{"upstream_inference_cost":0.063696,"upstream_inference_prompt_cost":0.038516,"upstream_inference_completions_cost":0.02518},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":706,"image_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0}}}